gate.py reported a timed-out gate as a harness crash.

native-backend-gates run on 2a85979, job macos-swiftui. The visible tail:

  .github/scripts/appium-ios-swiftui-smoke.sh: line 120: 21226 Terminated: 15
  validation/backends/with-ios-simulator.sh: line 29: 4430 Terminated: 15
  Exception in thread Thread-1 (stream_output):
  Traceback (most recent call last):
    File ".../validation/backends/gate.py", line 89, in stream_output
      while chunk := read_chunk(64 * 1024):
  ValueError: I/O operation on closed file
  ##[error]Process completed with exit code 1.

The Terminated: 15 lines are the real cause, two hundred lines above. The
traceback is the timeout path closing the pipe under a thread still blocked
in read(). The VERDICT was never wrong: execute() already returned
"timed-out" and the gate still failed. This is an attribution defect only.

After the guard, a child that outlives its timeout:

  execute returned: timed-out exit: None
  captured output: starting

HONEST LIMIT. The CI race did not reproduce on this machine: a timed-out
child returns "timed-out" with and without the guard locally, so the check
above proves the timeout path still reports correctly, NOT that it
reproduces the traceback. The guard is correct by construction, since a
closed pipe means there is nothing left to stream, rather than proven
against the failure it was written for. Recorded as unproven rather than
presented as fixed.

FOLLOW-UP: the declared environment was decorative.

process-capsule-longrun and process-checkpoint share one script and differ
only by REPROIT_CHECKPOINT_SCOPE, which the workflow set on the command line
while the gate entry ALSO declared it in an 'environment' block that gate.py
never read. Invoking the narrow gate by name alone therefore ran the WIDE
harness and then failed its requiredOutput:

  python3 validation/backends/gate.py process-capsule-longrun   -> exit 1

gate.py now applies a gate's declared environment to the child. After:

  process-capsule-longrun -> exit 0, NOT EVALUATED lines printed
  process-checkpoint      -> exit 0, all 7 cases

and the workflow no longer has to remember the variable. A declaration the
runner ignores is the same failure as a gate that cannot be scheduled: it
reads as configuration and behaves as nothing.
